\details{
Maps how well connected each cell is to its targets, as effective
resistance: to the habitat within \code{radius} (\code{source_strength}), or, with a
\code{destination}, between the two distributions in both directions. Where
\code{\link[=dp_omni]{dp_omni()}} and \code{\link[=dp_flux]{dp_flux()}} map where movement passes, this maps how
isolated each place is. Every cell with targets in reach gets a value,
not only habitat, so it also shows how connected a site would be there.

A cell's targets are the cells of its connected component within
\code{radius} and beyond its own site (see "Sites"; for single cells, the
other cells), each weighted by its share \eqn{q_j} of the cell's target
strength in reach, \eqn{A_i = \sum_{j \notin S_i} t_j k(d_{ij})}, where
\eqn{t} is the target strength, \eqn{S_i} is the cell's site, and
\eqn{k} is 1 within \code{radius} and 0 otherwise. With \eqn{R_{ij}} the effective resistance between the sites
around cells \eqn{i} and \eqn{j} (see "Sites") on the whole circuit, two
layers summarise the targets:
\describe{
\item{\code{resistance}}{Resistance to the target distribution: the
effective resistance when one unit of current leaves the site and
is withdrawn across its targets in proportion to \eqn{q}. Many
targets in reach act like parallel paths and lower it, so it measures
how well the site is connected to its targets as a set. It is the
injection of \code{\link[=dp_flux]{dp_flux()}} with the one site as the origin.}
\item{\code{mean_resistance}}{Mean effective resistance to the targets,
\eqn{\sum_j q_j R_{ij}}: the difficulty of reaching a typical target.
It is at least \code{resistance}, by the spread of the targets among
themselves, and the targets that are hardest to reach weigh most in
it. With \code{site_radius = 0}, summed over cells with weights
\eqn{w_i A_i}, it gives the total effective resistance over the pairs
of \code{\link[=dp_omni]{dp_omni()}}.}
}

With a \code{destination}, each cell is scored in both directions, with
\code{origin_} and \code{destination_} prefixes on the layers. The \code{origin_}
layers take the cell as an origin, with the destination
(\code{destination}) as its targets: how well could movement from here reach
the destination? The \code{destination_} layers take the cell as a
destination, with the origin (\code{source_strength}) as its targets: how
reachable is this place from the origin? For present and future habitat,
the first asks which populations can track their habitat, the second
which future habitat can be colonised. Effective resistance is
symmetric, so the two differ only in their targets.

Cells without targets in reach (none within \code{radius} and beyond their
site, in the same component) are NA. These include cells of pieces of
the circuit not much wider than a site, such as small islands, whose
site holds the whole piece.
\subsection{Sites}{

Each cell stands for a site: the cells within \code{site_radius} of it (2
pixels by default), across which the current is spread evenly. Targets
are sites too, so \eqn{R_{ij}} above is the effective resistance between
the sites around \eqn{i} and \eqn{j}. A site is never its own target:
a cell's targets are the habitat beyond its site, as a single cell's are
the other cells, so \code{radius} must reach beyond \code{site_radius}. This matters because on a grid,
the effective resistance from a single cell grows only with the log of
distance, so most of it is in the first few rings of links around the
cell. A single cell's value is then dominated by its own contact
resistance, the resistance of its own links in parallel, and maps the
cell's own resistance more than its place in the landscape. Spreading the
current over a site keeps that local term from dominating, as
Circuitscape's focal regions do. Choose \code{site_radius} for the area a
population or site occupies; values depend on it, so compare runs at the
same site radius.

With \code{site_radius = 0}, each site is its single cell, and its own
resistance is part of its isolation: the reading for a single point of
interest. With \code{contact = TRUE}, the result includes the \code{contact} layer,
each cell's contact resistance (exact, not estimated), which is a lower
bound on the single-cell \code{resistance}. Subtracting it leaves the
resistance beyond the cell's own links, but the subtraction doesn't
remove any noise, so where contact is most of the value (high-resistance
cells), the remainder is very noisy.
}

\subsection{Accessibility}{

\code{resistance} responds to where the targets are, not to how much of them
there is: \eqn{q} is a share, so thinning every target to a tenth of its
strength leaves it unchanged. With \code{accessibility = TRUE}, two layers
measure how much of the targets each cell can access, as area:
\describe{
\item{\code{target_area}}{The targets in reach as area, each cell's area
weighted by its target strength: \eqn{\sum_{j \notin S_i} t_j a_j
    k(d_{ij})}, with \eqn{a_j} the cell's area. For a 0-1 suitability it
is the area of suitable habitat in reach; for strengths above 1 it is
an area-equivalent, and can exceed the area in reach. It is exact,
and ignores resistance.}
\item{\code{accessible_area}}{\code{target_area} counted by how easily the
targets are reached, \eqn{\mathrm{target\_area}_i \, R^* / R_i},
where \eqn{R_i} is \code{resistance} and \eqn{R^*} is the resistance a
site would have in continuous habitat (every cell a target, of
resistance \code{reference_resistance}) at the same \code{radius} and
\code{site_radius}. Targets as easy to reach as in continuous habitat
count in full, and harder ones proportionally less; targets close to
the site, or through resistance below the reference, can count for
more. It responds to the amount of targets, their arrangement and the
resistance surface together. Its relative noise is that of
\code{resistance}, and it is corrected for the bias of inverting a noisy
estimate.}
}

Both are in square kilometres where the grid's
coordinate reference system gives cell areas, and in cells otherwise; the
\code{"area_units"} attribute says which. Both are computed from per-row
running sums, at a cost linear in the radius.

\eqn{R^*} is solved exactly, once per kernel, on a uniform grid wide
enough that its edges don't matter, and is proportional to
\code{reference_resistance}: set it to the resistance of good habitat. On
lon/lat grids with \code{radius_units = "km"}, the kernel varies by row, and
so does \eqn{R^*}; rows within reach of the top or bottom of the grid use
the nearest row whose kernel fits, so that there, as at other edges, the
grid's edge lowers accessible area rather than the reference.
Accessibility needs a finite \code{radius}.

A larger \code{radius} raises \code{target_area} everywhere and \code{accessible_area}
almost everywhere, but not at every cell: \code{resistance} withdraws current
from the targets in fixed proportions, so where widening the radius adds
mostly hard-to-reach targets, it raises the resistance to all of them, and
accessible area can fall. To compare runs at different resolutions, give
\code{site_radius} in km too: the default of 2 pixels is a different site on
each grid.
}

\subsection{Method}{

Each draw injects a random-sign dipole across every link of the circuit,
scaled by the square root of the link's conductance, and solves the
circuit once. For the solution \eqn{y}, \eqn{(y_i - y_j)^2} is then an
unbiased estimate of \eqn{R_{ij}} for every pair at once, and both layers
come from sums of \eqn{y} and \eqn{y^2} over each cell's targets, after
averaging \eqn{y} over each site. Their mean over draws estimates each
layer without bias.

Flow potential has no direct analogue here. With \code{normalize}, the same
draws on the uniform circuit (conductance 1 on the same cells) give the
same quantities for geometry alone, and the normalized layers divide by
them: the resistance of a unit-resistance landscape of the same shape
that would give the same isolation, so in the units of the input's
resistance. A normalized value of 2 means the cell is as isolated as it
would be if every cell had resistance 2. Above the landscape's typical
resistance, the cell is isolated by its resistances; below, by geometry
(distance, edges, few targets) more than by resistance.
}

\subsection{Accuracy}{

Each draw's estimate of \code{resistance} is a squared Gaussian-like quantity,
so its relative standard error is about \eqn{\sqrt{2 / \mathrm{n\_draws}}}
at any grid size: about 18\% at the default 64 draws, and 10\% at 200.
\code{mean_resistance} averages over targets and is usually less noisy. Both
circuits see the same draws, so on landscapes of modest contrast the
noise of the normalized layers largely cancels, but where resistance
varies by orders of magnitude it doesn't. With \code{se = TRUE}, the result includes each layer's per-cell
standard error, from the spread of the draws; the \code{"relative_se"}
attribute gives each layer's median relative standard error either way.

Reproducibility, performance and progress are as for \code{\link[=dp_omni]{dp_omni()}}: one
factorization (two with \code{normalize}), then about \code{n_draws} solves.
Accessibility adds a small factorization and one solve per kernel.
}
}
